# AI Agent Coding Standards

## Coding Style and Format
- Use consistent naming conventions (e.g., utility functions start with verbs, e.g., `fetchData`, `processInput`)
- Use descriptive names for agent components that reflect their functionality and responsibilities (e.g., `InputProcessor`, `DecisionEngine`)
- Keep functions and methods concise (no more than 50 lines)
- Use meaningful variable names (avoid names like agent1, agent2)
- Use consistent indentation and formatting (4-space indentation is recommended)
- Split complex logic into smaller functions
- Limit the number of function parameters (ideally no more than 3)

## Code Comments and Documentation
- Provide clear comments for complex agent behaviors
- Document the design intent and parameters of prompt templates
- Add clear docstrings to utility functions that explain inputs, outputs, and usage
- Add explanatory comments at key decision points
- Use consistent comment style and formatting
- Code should be self-explanatory, and comments should explain "why" rather than just "what"
- Provide high-level documentation for each major component of the proxy system

## Modularity and component design
- Decompose proxy functionality into independent reusable components
- Clearly define interfaces between components
- Separate prompt templates from business logic
- Avoid tightly coupled proxy design
- Implement separation of concerns (SoC) principle
- Components should have a single responsibility
- Define clear dependencies and data flows

## Error handling
- Implement robust error handling mechanisms
- Use appropriate error handling for API calls and external tools
- Provide graceful degradation strategies for proxy failures
- Log errors and exceptions for debugging
- Distinguish between different types of errors (user errors, system errors, third-party service errors)
- Provide meaningful error messages and recovery suggestions

## Code quality tools
- Use static code analysis tools (ESLint, Pylint, etc.)
- Implement code style checkers (such as Black, Prettier)
- Use type checking (TypeScript, mypy, etc.)
- Integrate code quality checks into CI/CD processes
- Perform regular code reviews
- Establish quantitative metrics and goals for code quality

## AI-specific code considerations
- Strategies for managing context window size limits
- Coding practices to optimize token usage
- Safeguards against model hallucinations
- Implement request retry and fallback strategies
- Caching mechanisms to reduce unnecessary model calls
- Implement post-processing logic for model outputs